1. Summary and Technical Explanation

The profile page exposes a “New Password (leave empty to keep current password)” field that lets an authenticated user change their account password without re-entering or verifying the current password. Submitting any value in this field immediately updates the account’s password to the new value.

Password-change functionality should always require proof of the current credential (the current password, or a recent re-authentication / step-up check) before accepting a new one. Without this check, the password field behaves as an unauthenticated write: anything that can make an authenticated request on the victim’s behalf — a hijacked session, an XSS payload, a stolen session cookie, or a CSRF request if the endpoint lacks CSRF tokens/SameSite protections — can silently change the account password and lock the real owner out, with no need to know the original password at all.
This significantly amplifies the impact of any other session-related weakness in the application: an attacker who only has temporary access to a session (e.g., a stolen cookie, a shared/forgotten logged-in device, or a short-lived XSS) can convert that temporary access into permanent, exclusive control of the account by resetting the password, even though they never knew or captured the original credential.

2. Steps to Reproduce
Log in to the application as a test account.
Navigate to https://geo-salmon-complement-walk.trycloudflare.com/profile.
Locate the “New Password (leave empty to keep current password)” field.
Enter a new password (e.g., NewPass123!) without providing the current/old password anywhere in the form.
Submit the form.
Log out.
Log back in using the new password (NewPass123!).

Expected result: The server rejects the change, or prompts for the current password / re-authentication before applying it.

Actual result: The password is changed successfully with no verification of the existing password.
3. Impact
Account lockout / takeover amplification: Any attacker who achieves even brief, unauthorized access to a victim’s authenticated session (via XSS, a leaked/stolen session cookie, a shared device, or a CSRF-able request) can permanently lock the legitimate owner out by setting a password only the attacker knows.
No re-authentication barrier: Sensitive account actions (password change, email change) performed without re-verifying identity violate the principle that high-impact changes require fresh proof of credentials.
